{
  "title": "Emerging Immunotherapy Study | Purdue Veterinary Medicine",
  "text": "Emerging Immunotherapy Study | Purdue Veterinary Medicine\nSkip to main content\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\nLeave Your Print\nDonate Today!\nMain Menu\nPVM Homepage\nApply\nDoctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM)\nVeterinary Technology Distance Learning\nVeterinary Technology On-Campus\nFinancial Aid\nBoiler Vet Camp\nVet Up!\nVeterinary Research Scholars Summer Program\nJob Opportunities\nAbout\nAbout Purdue Veterinary Medicine\nLeadership\nAcademic Departments\nAdministration & Service Units\nCommunity Engagement\nGlobal Engagement\nAwards & Recognition\nFaculty & Staff Directory\nContact & Directions\nCareer Resources\nEducation\nEducational Programs\nDoctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM) Program\nVeterinary Technology Program\nUndergraduate & Post-Baccalaureate Programs\nGraduate & Postgraduate Programs\nInternship & Residency Programs\nContinuing Education\nStudents & Alumni\nAlumni Association\nAlumni Events\nCareer Resources\nCounseling & Wellness\nPVM Yearbook\nStudent Organizations\nStudent Success\nTranscripts\nVeterinary Medical Library\nHospitals & Laboratories\nEmergencies 24/7\nAnimal Disease Diagnostic Laboratory\nPurdue University Veterinary Hospital\nEquine Hospital\nFarm Animal Hospital\nSmall Animal Hospital\nCaesars Entertainment Equine Specialty Hospital - Shelbyville\nVeterinarians & Referrals\nHospital Virtual Tours\nClient Portal\nResearch\nOffice of Research\nSignature Research Programs\nResearch Centers & Labs\nOne Health\nVeterinary Clinical Trials\nGrant Proposals and Funding\nGiving\nNews\nPurdue Veterinary Medicine News\nCommunications & Public Affairs\nLeave a Print. Donate Today!\nWerling Comparative Oncology Research Center\nWCORC\nMenu\nAbout\nWhat is the WCORC?\nOur Team\nResidency\nAppointment Reminders\nBladder Cancer Screenings\nCancer Research\nCancer Research\nCanine Lymphoma Research\nCanine Urinary Bladder Cancer Research\nComparative Oncology Trials Consortium\nOncology Legacy Program\nClinical Trials\nClinical Trials\nCanine Lymphomas\nCanine Urinary Bladder Cancer\nCanine Osteosarcoma\nEmerging Canine Cancer Studies\nTumor Ablation\nEmerging Immunotherapy Study\nClinical Trials FAQ\nNews\nDonate\nClinical Trials\nCanine Lymphomas\nCanine Urinary Bladder Cancer\nCanine Osteosarcoma\nEmerging Canine Cancer Studies\nTumor Ablation\nEmerging Immunotherapy Study\nClinical Trials FAQ\nEmerging Immunotherapy Study\nSHP-1 Inhibitor (M029) Phase I Clinical Trial in Dogs with Cancer\nPrincipal Investigators\nDr. Marejka Shaevitz and Dr. Debbie Knapp, Purdue University College of Veterinary Medicine\nBackground\nThe immune system has a natural ability to recognize and attack cancer cells, but cancers can use several mechanisms to reduce this immune response. M029 is a new oral immunotherapy drug that blocks SHP-1, a protein that can limit immune system activity. By blocking SHP-1, researchers hope that M029 may allow the immune system to better recognize and attack cancer cells.\nM029 has been well tolerated in laboratory dogs, but this is the first clinical trial of M029 in pet dogs with naturally occurring cancer. The main goals of this Phase I study are to evaluate the safety of M029, learn how the drug moves through the body, determine whether it is having the expected effect on its target, and identify a dose that produces a biological effect. We will also look for any signs of anti-cancer activity in dogs receiving the drug.\nBecause M029 is a new, first-in-class drug, there may be side effects that are not yet known. Possible side effects include stomach upset and effects from an overactive immune system, such as inflammation of organs or worsening of an underlying autoimmune disease. Dogs enrolled in the trial will be monitored closely for these and other possible side effects.\nM029 is given by mouth once daily. The dose will be gradually increased if the dog tolerates treatment well. Dogs will return to Purdue Veterinary Hospital Oncology every two weeks at first and then monthly for examinations, bloodwork, tumor measurements, and other monitoring. Some visits will last most of the day because several blood samples will be collected to measure drug levels and the drug's effects in the body.\nEligibility for Dogs to Participate in the Study\nDogs may be eligible if they:\nAre at least 1 year old and weigh at least 10 kg (22 pounds)\nHave a cancer diagnosis confirmed by cytology or biopsy\nHave a tumor that can be measured during a physical examination or on x-rays\nHave not yet received treatment for their cancer\nAre otherwise healthy and expected to live at least 18 weeks\nCan take oral medication at home on an empty stomach\nExclusion Criteria\nDogs may not be eligible if they have:\nA history or evidence of autoimmune disease\nCertain endocrine, heart, or other significant diseases\nReceived chemotherapy or radiation therapy within the previous 3 months\nPreviously received immunotherapy\nRecently received steroids, NSAIDs, herbal products, CBD, or other supplements\nFinal eligibility will be determined by the study team after review of the dog's medical history and screening results.\nFinancial Support\nM029 is provided at no cost to owners. Owners are responsible for the costs of initial cancer diagnosis and staging before enrollment. Once enrolled, owners pay a flat fee of\n$350 per month\nto help cover bloodwork and study monitoring.\nIf an emergency visit is required because of a suspected treatment-related side effect, the study may provide up to $500 toward the cost of that visit. The study will also cover the cost of a necropsy if a participating dog dies or is euthanized.\nTrial Status\nActively Recruiting\nQuestions\nFor more information about the SHP-1 clinical trial or to find out whether your dog may be eligible, please contact\nDr. Marejka Shaevitz\nor the Clinical Trial Technician\nHolly Aschliman\nby calling\n765-494-1107\nor emailing\nSHP1Canine@purdue.edu\n.\nCommunication\nOneCampus Portal\nBrightspace\nBoilerConnect\nOffice 365\nOutlook\nmyPurdue\nCampus\nFaculty & Staff\nHuman Resources\nPurdue Careers\nColleges & Schools\nPurdue Directory\nCampus Map\nEducation\nVeterinary Medicine\nVeterinary Technology\nGraduate Programs\nResidency & Internships\nCommunity Engagement\nContinuing Education\nResources\nIntranet\nPVM Careers\nPVM Connect\nPVM Directory\nVeterinary Medical Library\nHospital & Diagnostic Services\nLast Updated: October 2, 2026\nPurdue University College of Veterinary Medicine, 625 Harrison Street, West Lafayette, IN 47907,\n765-494-7607\n© 2026 Purdue University\nAll Rights Reserved |\nIntegrity Statement\n|\nEA/EO University\n|\nDOE Degree Scorecards\n|\nCopyright Complaints\n|\nPrivacy Policy\nMaintained by Purdue Veterinary Medicine Communications\n. If you have trouble accessing this page because of a disability, please\nreport an accessibility issue\n.\nBy using our website you agree to our\nprivacy policy\n.\nI Understand",
